{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And He gave to Moses, etc.</b> There is no earlier and later in the Torah — the incident of the calf preceded the commandment of the work of the Mishkan by many days. For on the 17th of Tammuz the tablets were broken, and on Yom HaKippurim the Holy One, blessed be He, was reconciled to Israel, and the next day they began the freewill-offering for the Mishkan, and it was erected on the first of Nisan (Tanchuma)." "<b>When He finished.</b> ככלתו is written deficient — that the Torah was given to him as a gift, like a bride to the groom, for he could not have learned all of it in so short a time. Another matter: just as a bride is adorned with twenty-four adornments — those mentioned in the book of Isaiah — so a sage must be expert in twenty-four books." "<b>To speak with him.</b> The statutes and the laws that are in 'These are the laws.'" "<b>To speak with him.</b> This teaches that Moses would hear from the mouth of the Gevurah, and they would go back and review the halachah, the two of them together." "<b>Tablets.</b> לחת is written [defective], that the two of them were equal (ibid.)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:56:01.758341104Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}